.PP
.B int _printf(const char *format, ..)

.B This is the function call, it is used for printing formatted output. It starts with a format string, which ncludes regular text %i, numbers %o & special characters %r. A format string/variable number containing arguments that fills the place holder and will print to the screen output.


.SH DESCRIPTION ->
Custom version of _printf with standard functions built into it.
.PP
.B Named my_printf, this version as it's parent reads the format string, dicerns data and prints it to the screen. Starts with va_start which opens a list, accesses the stored memory, then each value is obtained through va_arg. Both come from the library <stdarg.h>, that lets the functions handle any number of arguments.

.SH FORMAT SPECIFIERS
.TP
.B → %c
Prints a single character (letter or symbol).

.TP
.B → %s
Prints a string of characters (word or sentence).

.TP
.B → %d  %i
Prints a whole number (positive or negative).

.TP
.B → %u
Prints an unsigned (always positive number).

.TP
.B → %x
Prints a number in lowercase hexadecimal format (base 16).

.TP
.B → %X
Same as %x, but this prints hex in UPPERCASE.

.TP
.B → %p
Prints a pointer (memory address), in hex with 0x. Used for memory address.

.TP
.B → %r
Prints unknown spesifiers,& it is a reverse string.

.TP
.B → %%
Literal percent sign, prints a percent sign. Since % is a special character in format strings it's needed to escape it and print.


.SH FILES
.B Project is organized by files for modularity, 
.PP
.B  _printf.c 

Is the boss(dispatcher), this is the main controller of the operation. Uses switch-case for clean and organized way of checking a match for values and characters. It is also an easier way of applying new specifiers.
.PP
.B  main.h 

This is the header file, it is a log of what tools are accesable and what is int place to work and be applied through expected parameters.
.PP
.B print_text.c

This file contains 3 functions, it prints characters %c, strings %s and deals with weird inputs %r.
.PP

.B print_numbers.c 

This file has the function to print different types of numbers, like int %d %i, unsigned %u and octal %o.
.PP
.B print_hex_pointers.c

This file prints hexadecimal numbers (base 16 numbers) Using (args, 0) to call lowercase %x, (args, 1) to call UPPERCASE %X. It also handles memory addresses %p.


.SH BUGS 
Unknown specifiers like %r or %k are printed as-is.
Null strings passed to %s are printed as (null).

.B *Does not have the features to handle flags, width, precision or length modifiers.

